  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist. I haven't opened the dashboard to check how the panels render, and I haven't checked that the rule runs without errors (it showed status \"pending\" when created).\n\n**What the data holds:** the logs data view covers web requests, with `timestamp` as the time field. It includes:\n- `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as a string), `bytes`, `extension`, `clientip`, `url` and `request`.\n- `geo.src`, `geo.dest` and `geo.coordinates` for location.\n- `machine.os` and `agent` for client details.\n\n**Dashboard \"EFF-ff39ba95 Web logs overview\"** (id `eff-ff39ba95-web-logs-overview-e38240090351`) has four panels:\n- Total requests, as a single number.\n- Requests over time, as an area chart.\n- Requests by response code, as a pie chart.\n- Average bytes by file extension, as a bar chart.\n\nMy first attempt to create the dashboard failed because the time-series panel was missing its time field. I added `timestamp` and the second attempt worked.\n\n**Rule \"EFF-ff39ba95 5xx spike\"** (id `d679014a-cb20-4cfb-a51a-8cd20fef73f6`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It is enabled and checks every minute. It counts documents in `kibana_sample_data_logs` where `response.keyword` matches `5*`, over a 5-minute window. It fires when the count is above 10.\n\nThe rule has no notification action attached, so it will only record alerts in Kibana.",
